WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:11.550
Hi. I'm Ryan with Healing Headstones.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:13.447
I'm going to walk you
through how I scrape,

00:00:13.447 --> 00:00:15.763
scrub, and deep clean
moss-covered headstones.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:21.418
I start by assessing the stone

00:00:21.418 --> 00:00:23.970
and make sure, especially if it's older,

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:25.470
if it can handle a deep clean.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:28.530
The dirtiest parts are the
very base at the ground

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:30.243
or also deep in the letters.

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:34.402
The letters seem to hold on
to the most dirt and grime

00:00:34.402 --> 00:00:36.420
because they're cut into the stone.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:37.770
Most of the newer stones are made

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:39.330
of either marble or granite.

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:41.550
They have more of a glossy finish.

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:44.250
The stones before the
'20s, they don't have,

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.470
if they did have gloss,
it's not there anymore.

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:48.840
So I think that's why
they get stained way worse

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.060
and it's way deeper into the stone.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:52.085
The first thing I do is

00:00:52.085 --> 00:00:53.501
spray down the stone with water

00:00:53.501 --> 00:00:55.080
to make the moss removal easier.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:59.040
I use a hand-pump sprayer
to wet the headstones

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:00.898
and also to spray them off.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.180
I'll start scraping
the moss top to bottom,

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:10.920
'cause that's the easiest method.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.860
And you can see on the
bottom layers of moss,

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.380
it turns into almost a
concrete-like material,

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.930
which makes it even harder
to get off and remove it.

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:19.980
I use a special scraper.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.500
It's kind of a plastic and
rubber bill at the same time,

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.003
so it's made for headstones.

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:27.000
I'll use the corner of the scraper

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:28.808
to get into the small crevices

00:01:28.808 --> 00:01:30.974
and remove any other debris.

00:01:32.861 --> 00:01:33.900
Now, when you're using the scraper,

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.990
you have to be careful of
the condition of the stone,

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:40.230
'cause some of them, they can
have what's called sugaring,

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:41.646
where it's almost, as you touch it,

00:01:41.646 --> 00:01:43.054
it'll turn into a powder.

00:01:43.054 --> 00:01:44.550
Same thing with cracks,

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:47.220
and the same thing with stability.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:48.386
With a scraper, you're not really

00:01:48.386 --> 00:01:49.836
trying to get all the moss,

00:01:49.836 --> 00:01:51.480
you're just trying to
get the top layer off

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.370
without damaging the stone.

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:55.290
You want to remove as
much moss as possible

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:58.210
so it's easier when you
start scrubbing the stone.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:00.900
The black is mold and moss buildup.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.640
It can also be green.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.799
Sometimes you'll see
orange, but that's more,

00:02:04.799 --> 00:02:06.792
they have the metal
rods holding the stone,

00:02:06.792 --> 00:02:08.340
and that's where water's
gotten on the inside

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.620
and the rust starts to run out.

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:12.150
And that can stain them as well.

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:14.070
Sometimes you can scrape the moss off

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:15.660
and you can find mold underneath,

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:18.960
which has stained it black
or green into the stone.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:20.520
I'd say the moss on this stone

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.910
has been built up for minimum five years.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:26.340
Environment and climate
can both have an effect

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:28.617
on the growth that's on the stone.

00:02:28.617 --> 00:02:29.850
The finish of the stone

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:32.850
can affect how much grows on it,

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:34.020
what grows on it.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.709
The rougher finishes can hold water,

00:02:36.709 --> 00:02:40.029
and it holds algae and
stuff that builds up,

00:02:40.029 --> 00:02:42.630
which kind of just eventually
will take over the stone.

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:43.905
With a smooth surface,

00:02:43.905 --> 00:02:46.213
even the rain can wash away the growth

00:02:46.213 --> 00:02:47.490
or anything that starts to build up there

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:49.406
before it ever takes a hold.

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:56.940
Once all the moss and
debris is off the stone,

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:59.370
I spray down the stone with D/2 cleaner.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:01.260
After I let the spray cleaning solution

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:02.760
set for about 10 minutes,

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.040
I'll follow that up with
a soft-bristle brush.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.440
And I have found that
using a circular pattern

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.023
is the best method.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:14.700
The technique can vary
depending on the stone design

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:17.610
and the engravings on
the front of the stone.

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:19.230
I'll sometimes use a toothbrush

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.450
to get down into the smaller crevices.

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:24.600
It just helps to more
thoroughly get all the debris

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:26.350
and things out of the small places.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:31.380
I'll use a wooden pick
to go inside the letters

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:34.170
and some of the engraving
if it's a deep, small area

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:35.970
that a brush can't really get into.

00:03:39.524 --> 00:03:41.640
To get it back to its original state

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:44.256
or as close as you can,
sometimes you have to do

00:03:44.256 --> 00:03:46.453
certain parts of the
process over and over.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:55.080
I'll do a final spray off

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:57.690
to make sure all the
debris is off the stone.

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:00.210
I finish the cleaning
with a protective coating

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:01.590
of the cleaner, 'cause it continues

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:04.710
to keep anything from growing
or building up on the stone.

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:06.450
I would recommend checking a stone

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:08.340
at least once every six months, minimum,

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:10.800
to make sure nothing
has started building up,

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.693
before it gets to an excessive level.

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:19.560
It's rewarding to be able
to help these families

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:23.044
and also honor those whose
graves are left unmaintained.

